Rosalind,<br />
<br />
Kinnaird's letter does everything we asked on the 103 accounts and nothing =
at all outside them, and I want to be explicit about that before we close r=
ather than discover it later.<br />
<br />
The undertaking is drafted by account rather than by practice. It says the =
firm will fix the accounts on Schedule A. It does not say the firm will sto=
p using fee-debit authorizations, and it does not reach an account opened n=
ext year on the old form of agreement. Thackston clearly drafted it that wa=
y on purpose, and to be fair to her, she told us she was doing it.<br />
<br />
My view is that we take it anyway. The remediation is real, the schedule is=
 reconciled to the billing system, and the revised form of agreement they a=
re re-papering onto has no withdrawal authority in it, which functionally c=
overs new business as long as they keep using it. But the coverage of new b=
usiness comes from their form, not from their undertaking, and those are di=
fferent things with different lifespans.<br />
<br />
Please note the distinction in the closing summary. If somebody reads this =
file in five years I would like the schedule limitation to be the first thi=
ng they see, not something they work out.<br />
<br />
Priya